# No lazy deferral

Work that is scoped to a PR lands in that PR. Deferring scoped work is
sometimes necessary — but it is a **decision with required form**, never
a default an implementer reaches for because the commit grew, the item
is tedious, or the consumer "can land later."

## The rule

A deferral of scoped work is legitimate only when all three hold:

1. **A named blocking reason exists.** "Blocked on X" where X is a
   concrete, checkable dependency: an unlanded harness, an external
   standardization, a gated upstream PR, a review decision not yet
   made. "The commit was getting big" is a *split* reason, not a
   *deferral* reason — a split re-schedules the work inside the same
   PR; a deferral moves it out. "It felt separable" is neither.
2. **The deferral is tagged and scheduled.** A `docs/FOLLOWUPS.md`
   entry (or an amendment to the PR's own plan doc) with the blocker
   named, a target version/PR, and the reopening criterion per
   `21-reversion-clause-discipline.mdc`. An allow-comment pointing at
   a future consumer is *not* a deferral record — it is a residue
   marker that a deferral record must exist elsewhere to justify.
3. **The deferral is disclosed in the flow that made it.** The commit
   message or the report to the reviewer for the commit that sheds the
   work states: what was scoped, what is deferred, why, and where it
   lands. A deferral discovered later by a re-scope audit — instead of
   disclosed by the commit that made it — is a process failure even
   when the deferral itself would have been approved.

## The motivating example

PR-3 commit 4 (`AssembleEmissionClaim`, 2026-07-11): the plan scoped
the claim orchestration into commit 4 ("the C-4 claim orchestration is
the sole production construction site," `backing_set.rs`, written at
commit 3). Commit 4 grew, the handler was split out — a defensible
split — but the orchestration half was silently re-pointed to "the
PR-3 Engine-side claim orchestrator" in allow comments, with no plan
amendment, no FOLLOWUPS entry, and no disclosure in the commit report.
The deferral surfaced only in the commit-5 re-scope audit. The PR row's
own deliverable ("first live consumer — un-deadens GF-4b §5 item 1")
was false in the interim. The disposition: the orchestrator lands in
the same PR, as its own commit; the deferral-without-disclosure is the
anti-pattern this rule names.

## Why lazy deferral compounds

Each silent deferral looks small. In aggregate they produce a FOLLOWUPS
queue that is months of work whose items nobody decided to defer — the
queue *accumulated* rather than being *chosen*. Per
`15-deletion-and-debt.mdc`, FOLLOWUPS is not a graveyard: the V3.0
pre-genesis queue is load-bearing, and every silent addition to it is
un-reviewed scope movement on the critical path. The deferral decision
belongs to the human reviewer, made on disclosure — not to the
implementer, made by omission.

## Callee-without-caller is the canonical smell

A PR that lands a callee (handler, verifier, type, message) whose
production caller is "a future commit" has deferred integration work.
Sometimes staged landing is the plan (inert-until-wired is a legitimate
pattern in this repo — see the 2c-2a/2c-2b chain). The difference is
whether the staging is **in the plan** (named commits, named consumers,
reviewed sequence) or **emerged silently** (the caller was scoped here
and quietly became "later"). Staging allows with named consumers are
the marker of the first; allow comments whose named consumer shifted
between commits without a plan change are the marker of the second.

## A deferral's CONDITION can expire, and expiry looks exactly like pending

A disclosed deferral is usually written as *"skip this if X lands first"*.
That is the right shape. What it does not carry is a way to notice that
**X never landed** — because a condition that has expired and a condition
that is still pending read identically. The deferral just sits there, and
the next reader takes "blocked" from a line that only ever said "blocked
on X".

Observed: `COVER_TRAFFIC_RESTORATION.md` §2.9 step 4 deferred wiring the
carrier shim with *"or skip if step 5 lands first"*. Step 5 was then ruled
blocked on a daemon/p2p cutover that has no design doc. Nobody re-read the
skip, so a deferral taken on a condition that never fired became a permanent
block on the mechanism — and "the carrier is blocked" was read off a line
whose actual subject was *deleting the C++ relay path*, which is a different
thing that really is blocked.

**So:**

- **State the condition as something that can be observed to have failed**,
  not only to have succeeded. *"Skip if X lands first"* has no failure
  reading; *"skip if X lands first; if X is still open when this file is
  next touched, the skip is void"* does.
- **A deferral is not discharged by the passage of time.** When you touch a
  file carrying one, re-check its condition before you inherit its
  conclusion — the condition, not the conclusion.
- **Do not read a block off a neighbouring statement.** "Deleting A is
  blocked" is not "A cannot run". Check what the blocked item's subject
  actually is before scoping around it.

Every `21-reversion-clause-discipline.mdc` reopening criterion has this
shape, which is why the check belongs in the rule rather than in one
document: a reopen criterion that nobody re-evaluates is a permanent
rejection wearing a conditional's clothes.

## Interaction with existing rules

- **`15-deletion-and-debt.mdc`** — "while we're here" governs scope
  *additions*; this rule governs scope *sheddings*. Both move work
  across the PR boundary without review; both are named so the review
  catches them.
- **`21-reversion-clause-discipline.mdc`** — a legitimate deferral is
  a reversion-clause-shaped record: rejected-for-now with named
  reopening criteria. A lazy deferral is the "we'll think about it
  later" anti-pattern that rule already forbids; this rule adds the
  disclosure-in-flow requirement.
- **`06-branching.mdc`** — the 5-day/10-commit ceiling creates the
  pressure to shed scope. The sanctioned relief valves are splitting
  the PR (with a plan) or an explicit, disclosed deferral — never a
  silent one.
- **`90-commits.mdc`** — the commit that defers references what it
  defers, the same way it references the spec section it implements.
